Official library containing the database for [URL="https://www.esoui.com/downloads/info1617-FurnitureCatalogue.html"]FurnitureCatalogue[/URL]. It is required for FurnitureCatalogue to work.

[COLOR=red][SIZE=4]Database updates ship in this library, independent of FurnitureCatalogue releases.[/SIZE][/COLOR]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=6]AddOns that use this Lib[/SIZE][/COLOR]
[LIST]
[*][URL="https://www.esoui.com/downloads/info1617-FurnitureCatalogue.html"]FurnitureCatalogue[/URL]
[*][URL="https://www.esoui.com/downloads/info1865-FurnitureShoppingListFurC2.0patch.html"]FurnitureShoppingList (FurC 2.0 patch)[/URL][/LIST]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=6]Adds Commands[/SIZE][/COLOR]

[highlight="lua"]---Everything about an item: its sources (and its mats if craftable)
/lfc <ITEMLINK OR ID>

---The raw record, with the enums named
/lfc raw <ITEMLINK OR ID>

---Database status: library version, state, revision, item count
/lfc db[/highlight]Both item commands take an item link or a plain item id, so [B]/lfc 203600[/B] and [B]/lfc |H1:item:203600:...|h|h[/B] answer the same.

[B]/lfc raw[/B] prints the records the API hands out, with the enums named - one line per source, ranked:

[code]/lfc raw 203600
LFC: 203600: [Scribing Altar]
LFC: primary=VENDOR version=BASE43 cat=25(Services)/104(Crafting Stations)
LFC: sources=PVP,ROLIS,VENDOR
LFC: [1] type=VENDOR vendor=SI_FURC_TRADERS_AF achievement=3985 location=1436(Infinite Archive) cost=30000/12
LFC: [2] type=ROLIS vendor=SI_FURC_TRADERS_FAUSTINA achievement=3985 place=SI_FURC_LOC_ANY_CAPITAL cost=800/4
LFC: [3] type=PVP vendor=SI_FURC_TRADERS_AF achievement=3985 location=181(Cyrodiil) cost=1000000/2[/code]


[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=6]For Devs and Contributors[/SIZE][/COLOR]

[URL="https://github.com/wookiefriseur/LFC"]Repo on GitHub[/URL]


This page is just the short version. Check out [B]LibFurnitureCatalogue/Api.lua[/B] and [URL="https://wookiefriseur.github.io/LFC/"]GH Pages site[/URL] for the full API reference and the contributor web interface


[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=6]API[/SIZE][/COLOR]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Initialise[/SIZE][/COLOR]

Declare it in your manifest, then reach it as a global. All endpoints are on the [B]LibFurnitureCatalogue.API[/B] table.

Probe that table to test if the lib is available. Do [B]not[/B] probe the [B]FurC[/B] global, because it belongs to FC and does not tell you anything about the state of the library.

[code]## DependsOn: LibFurnitureCatalogue>=1002000[/code][highlight="lua"]if not (LibFurnitureCatalogue and LibFurnitureCatalogue.API) then return end
local LFC = LibFurnitureCatalogue.API

LFC.OnReady(function()
  d(LFC.GetEntryCount() .. " furnishings")
end)
[/highlight]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Is it ready yet?[/SIZE][/COLOR]

The build is asynchronous. Any endpoint that reads the database starts it. An early call answers nil / false / {} / 0, which could be mistaken for an "item not found". One [B]OnReady[/B] is enough to avoid that. Wrap the first use so you don't query an empty database, then reach LFC from anywhere.

[B]IsReady[/B] answers false while the build runs and after a fail, and it deliberately [B]does not start[/B] the build. If you only poll [B]IsReady[/B], it will wait forever unless something else kicks off the build (use [B]OnReady[/B] once, lazily, on your first query)

[B]GetState[/B] showing BUILDING means it will finish and showing FAILED means it will not, until somebody rebuilds. A waiter registered while FAILED is refused rather than queued.

[highlight="lua"]local state, err = LFC.GetState()
if state == LFC.State.FAILED then d("catalogue failed to build: " .. tostring(err)) end
[/highlight]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Where does this item come from?[/SIZE][/COLOR]
Every call hands out a fresh copy, yours to keep and to mutate

[highlight="lua"]local src = LFC.GetSourceTypes()
local entry = LFC.GetEntry(203600) -- item link, blueprint link or itemId

if entry then
  entry.version --> 32, the update it arrived in

  -- sources is a SET, so membership is one lookup
  entry.sources          --> { [6] = true, [7] = true, [13] = true }
  entry.sources[src.PVP] --> true

  if entry.blueprint then d("craftable") end
end
[/highlight]

Nil means the catalogue does not have it. [B]LFC.Has(itemId)[/B] answers the same question without the copy.

All entries have the attributes [B]id[/B], [B]sources[/B], [B]version[/B] and [B]blueprint[/B] (if craftable). Anything else you find on it is internal and may move. There is no field saying which source is the best one, but the records arrive ranked.

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Get all of it, one record per source[/SIZE][/COLOR]
You get identifiers, so it reads the same on every client language. The list is ranked best first. Rank order is usually by source availability (drop is easier to get than a specific crown crate, etc)

[highlight="lua"]for _, record in ipairs(LFC.GetSourceDetails(203600)) do
  record.source.type              --> 6, then 13, then 7
  GetString(record.source.vendor) --> "Faustina Curio" on the writ vendor one


  -- every place this source covers, always a list
  for _, placement in ipairs(record.source.locations or {}) do
    if placement.location then GetZoneNameById(placement.location) end
    if placement.place then GetString(placement.place) end
  end

  if record.cost then
    record.cost.currency --> 12, then 4, then 2
    record.cost.amount   --> 30000, then 800, then 1000000
  end

  record.lastSeen --> "2026-05-01" on luxury furnisher records, nil everywhere else
end
[/highlight]

Full example for the Scribing Altar, sold three ways:

[highlight="lua"]LFC.GetEntry(203600)
local result = {
    id = 203600,
    version = 32, -- GetDataVersionKeys()[32] == "BASE43", Update 43
    sources = { [6]=true, [7]=true, [13]=true }
    -- a craftable also carries `blueprint = 1234`
}

LFC.GetSourceDetails(203600)
local result = {
    -- Source #1 --
    { source = {
        type = 6, -- VENDOR
        vendor = SI_FURC_TRADERS_AF, -- "Achievement Furnisher"
        achievement = 3985, -- "Inheritor of the Scholarium"
        locations = { {location = 1436} } -- zoneId of the Infinite Archive
      },
      cost = { currency = 12, amount = 30000 } -- CURT_ARCHIVAL_FORTUNES
    },
    -- Source #2 --
    { source = {
        type = 13, -- ROLIS, the writ vendors
        vendor = SI_FURC_TRADERS_FAUSTINA, -- "Faustina Curio"
        achievement = 3985,
        locations = { {place = SI_FURC_LOC_ANY_CAPITAL} } -- "any capital city", no zoneId names it
      },
      cost = { currency = 4, amount = 800 } -- CURT_WRIT_VOUCHERS
    },
    -- Source #3 --
    { source = {
        type = 7, -- PVP
        vendor = SI_FURC_TRADERS_AF,
        achievement = 3985,
        locations = { {location = 181} } -- Cyrodiil
      },
      cost = { currency = 2, amount = 1000000 } -- CURT_ALLIANCE_POINTS
    },
}
[/highlight]

An empty list means the item is not ours [B]or[/B] the database is not built yet (use [B]IsReady[/B] if you need to tell them apart). Every item the catalogue contains carries at least one source.

Not every source has a price. [B]cost[/B] is carried by vendor, luxury, writ vendor, PvP, bazaar, crown store, housing editor, festival and crafting records, but a drop or a quest reward never has one.

Three fields use [B]0[/B] as an id, which means "we know it exists, but we cannot name it": [B]achievement[/B], [B]crate[/B] and [B]quest[/B]. Treat 0 as that state, not an id to look up.

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Turning ids into text[/SIZE][/COLOR]

Our strings carry ESO control characters, so a bare [B]GetString[/B] puts "^Nd,from" on the screen. Use [B]zo_strformat[/B] to format them.

Some are formats rather than names and take the thing they describe as [B]<<1>>[/B]: [B]bundle[/B] ("Crown Store bundle: <<1>>") and [B]itemPack[/B] ("Part of the Tamriel Tomes item pack [<<1>>]"). Prepositions live in their own strings, so if you want "in Wrothgar" rather than "Wrothgar" you join the two yourself, you can do it.

[highlight="lua"]zo_strformat("<<1>>", GetString(record.source.vendor)) --> "Luxury Furnisher"
[/highlight]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Get Mats[/SIZE][/COLOR]
Keyed by ingredient link, because that is what the game hands back and you need it to render a name anyways. Empty table when it is not craftable.

[highlight="lua"]for ingredientLink, quantity in pairs(LFC.GetIngredients(itemId)) do
  d(quantity .. "x " .. GetItemLinkName(ingredientLink)) --> "6x Banana"
end
[/highlight]

This reads the recipe from the game, not from our database, so it needs no ready database:

[highlight="lua"]LFC.GetIngredients(itemLink, LFC.GetEntry(itemId))
[/highlight]

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Use Enums, not numbers[/SIZE][/COLOR]
The numeric values can change, so use the enums for comparison instead of storing numbers in your AddOn. Iterate [B]ItemSources[/B] in Constants.lua.

[highlight="lua"]LFC.GetSourceTypes()      -- key -> id, for comparing
LFC.GetSourceTypeInfo()   -- id -> stable key and English label, for exports
LFC.GetDataVersions()     -- update key -> id
LFC.GetDataVersionKeys()  -- id -> update key
[/highlight]

The [B]FurC.*[/B] data tables (MiscItemSources, LuxuryFurnisher, AchievementVendors, PVP, Rolis, Faustina) are internal and may change at any time, use [B]GetSourceDetails[/B].

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Rebuilds[/SIZE][/COLOR]
The database is rebuilt when the player rescans. Subscribe if you cache anything that has to be cleared.

[highlight="lua"]LFC.RegisterCallback(LFC.Events.SCAN_COMPLETE, function(revision)
  myAddon:Invalidate(revision)
end)
[/highlight]

[B]GetDBRevision[/B] is a DB change counter. Anything you cached from the catalogue should be dropped when it moves.

[COLOR=wheat][SIZE=3]Migration[/SIZE][/COLOR]

Coming from older FC or LFC versions or migrating away from using internal tables: old on the left and the alternative on the right. [B]FurC[/B] on the left is a global; [B]LFC[/B] on the right is your local API alias from the Initialise block ([B]local LFC = LibFurnitureCatalogue.API[/B]).

[highlight="lua"]FurC.Find(id)               --> LFC.GetEntry(id)          -- a copy, and nil on a miss instead of {}
FurC.GetItemId(x)           --> LFC.GetItemId(x)
FurC.GetItemLink(x)         --> LFC.GetItemLink(x)
FurC.GetIngredients(x)      --> LFC.GetIngredients(x)
FurC.GetItemDescription(x)  --> LFC.GetItemDescription(x)  -- deprecated: render from GetSourceDetails
FurC.GetMats(x)             --> LFC.GetIngredients(x)      -- and format the map yourself
LibFurnitureCatalogue.API.SourceType --> LFC.GetSourceTypes() -- a copy of your own
entry.origin                --> LFC.GetSourceDetails(id)[1].source.type  -- the list is ranked
entry.compatSources         --> gone: every source has a record of its own now
record.availability         --> entry.version, and record.lastSeen for the luxury date
record.source.location      --> record.source.locations    -- always a list of { location, place }
record.source.place         --> record.source.locations    -- the same list
[/highlight]

A placement naming both a zone and a place is one place inside that zone, not two places. And a crown-store item is now [B]one[/B] record carrying the whole offer - price, packs, bundle, crate, houses - instead of one record per part.

Every endpoint is documented in the API reference. Ask, or open an issue, if something you need is missing.
